=== 2. GAME RULES (the single source of truth) ===
- 60 levels. Every level has 5 questions: 1 MAIN + 4 ROOM questions (R1..R4), difficulty strictly decreasing: MAIN > R1 > R2 > R3 > R4.
- Correct MAIN answer -> advance to the next level (level + 1). Level 60 solved -> victory screen.
- Wrong MAIN answer -> player is pushed into Room 1 and gets its easier question.
- Wrong in Room k (k<4) -> pushed to Room k+1 (easier again).
- Wrong in Room 4 -> FULL RESET: player returns to Level 0 (attempts history kept for the leaderboard, progress zeroed).
- Correct in ANY room -> player returns to the main level. Make this a config flag in one file:
  RETURN_MODE = "same_question" | "next_level"  (default "next_level"; with "same_question" the main question is replaced by an unused alternate variant so it cannot be memorised).
- Per-question timer (configurable per room, default MAIN 90s, R1 75s, R2 60s, R3 45s, R4 30s). Timeout counts as a wrong answer.
- Hints: optional, max 1 per level, cost 15 seconds off the timer or a score penalty (configurable).
- Scoring: base points per level + time bonus + streak bonus; room recoveries give reduced points.

Implement the rules as a PURE, fully unit-tested state machine (no UI, no network): states MAIN, ROOM_1..ROOM_4, LEVEL_UP, RESET, VICTORY. Use XState or a hand-written reducer. Export transition(state, event) -> {nextState, effects[]}. Effects are things like PLAY_ANIMATION("fall"), PLAY_SOUND("boing"), SAVE_PROGRESS. Write Vitest tests covering: every transition, timeout, reset, level 60 victory, both RETURN_MODE values, and 1000 randomized play-throughs that never reach an invalid state.

